=== SIGNATURE ===
scan3(start_a, start_b, start_c, arr, lambda_a, lambda_b, lambda_c)

=== COMMENT ===
Like SCAN but carries THREE accumulators. For each item of arr it updates a, b and c together; lambda_a/lambda_b/lambda_c each receive (a, b, c, item) and return the next value of that accumulator. Returns a 3-column array of the running (a, b, c) after each item.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(start_a, start_b, start_c, arr, lambda_a, lambda_b, lambda_c, LET(
   vec, TOCOL(arr),
   seeded, REDUCE(HSTACK(start_a, start_b, start_c), vec, LAMBDA(acc, item, LET(
      cur_a, INDEX(acc, ROWS(acc), 1),
      cur_b, INDEX(acc, ROWS(acc), 2),
      cur_c, INDEX(acc, ROWS(acc), 3),
      VSTACK(acc, HSTACK(
         lambda_a(cur_a, cur_b, cur_c, item),
         lambda_b(cur_a, cur_b, cur_c, item),
         lambda_c(cur_a, cur_b, cur_c, item)
      ))
   ))),
   DROP(seeded, 1)
))

=== DESCRIPTION ===
A three-accumulator SCAN. Threads three independent accumulators through arr so you don't have to pack them into one.

Arguments:
  start_a, start_b, start_c - initial values of the three accumulators.
  arr      - the items to iterate over (flattened top-to-bottom, like TOCOL).
  lambda_a - LAMBDA(a, b, c, item) returning the next a (sees current a, b, c and the item).
  lambda_b - LAMBDA(a, b, c, item) returning the next b.
  lambda_c - LAMBDA(a, b, c, item) returning the next c.

Returns:
  A 3-column array with one row per item of arr: columns 1/2/3 are the running a/b/c AFTER that item is processed (matching SCAN's per-item semantics). Take the last row for the final triple.

All three new values are computed from the PRE-update a, b and c, so the update lambdas can reference each other's previous values. Accumulators should be scalars (numbers or text), like SCAN.

Example - running sum, running product, running count over {2;3;4}:
  scan3(0, 1, 0, {2;3;4}, LAMBDA(a,b,c,x, a+x), LAMBDA(a,b,c,x, b*x), LAMBDA(a,b,c,x, c+1))
  -> {2,2,1; 5,6,2; 9,24,3}

Note: builds the result by appending rows (order n^2), so it suits small/medium arrays. See scan2 for two accumulators.
